# go2cs behavioral TargetComparison tests compare transpiled C# to .cs.target
# golden files BYTE-FOR-BYTE (TargetComparisonTests.FileMatch). The converter
# preserves the Go source's LF inside multi-line string literals, so these files
# legitimately contain mixed CRLF/LF. With core.autocrlf=true git would rewrite
# those LFs to CRLF on checkout and break the byte comparison. Mark the affected
# projects' transpiled output and goldens as binary so their exact bytes survive.
src/tests/Behavioral/Solitaire/*.cs            -text
src/tests/Behavioral/Solitaire/*.cs.target     -text
src/tests/Behavioral/SortArrayType/*.cs        -text
src/tests/Behavioral/SortArrayType/*.cs.target -text
src/tests/Behavioral/StdLibInternalAbi/*.cs        -text
src/tests/Behavioral/StdLibInternalAbi/*.cs.target -text
src/tests/Behavioral/UntypedConstWideMask/main.cs -text
src/tests/Behavioral/UntypedConstWideMask/main.cs.target -text

# Phase-4 test DATA staged from GOROOT and read byte-for-byte by a converted test
# suite. Go ships these with LF; autocrlf would smudge them on checkout and change
# what the test reads (compress/gzip's TestIssue6550 base64-decodes this file).
src/core/**/testdata/*.base64 -text
# Marking the whole testdata tree also stops a validated-package sweep from
# reporting these fixtures as modified on every run.
src/core/**/testdata/** -text

# The converter EMBEDS these templates at compile time (embeddedTemplates.go) and
# assumes CRLF: writePackageInfoFile splits the package_info skeleton on "\r\n", so
# a converter built from an LF checkout (Linux/macOS, or Windows with autocrlf=false)
# never finds the section end-markers and log.Fatals on EVERY package conversion —
# and the emitted .csproj/package_info.cs bytes must not vary with the build host's
# checkout. Pin them to CRLF in every working tree.
src/go2cs/csproj-template.xml      eol=crlf
src/go2cs/test-csproj-template.xml eol=crlf
src/go2cs/package_info-template.txt eol=crlf
